1
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:10,860
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

2
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:18,900
the sacred strength of Alcinous rose up from his bed,
and up rose Zeus-born Odysseus, sacker of cities.

3
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:21,360
And the sacred strength of Alcinous led the way

4
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:25,740
to the Phaeacians' gathering-place,
built there beside the ships.

5
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:31,400
They came and sat down on the polished stones side
by side. And Pallas Athena went up and down the city

6
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:37,900
in the likeness of a herald of wise Alcinous,
devising the homecoming of great-hearted Odysseus,

7
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,900
and standing beside each man she spoke her word:

8
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:44,400
"This way, come — leaders and
counselors of the Phaeacians —

9
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:49,320
to the gathering-place, to learn about the stranger
who has come new to the house of wise Alcinous

10
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:53,420
after wandering the sea —
a man like the immortals in body."

11
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:56,740
So speaking she stirred the strength and spirit of each,

12
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:58,920
and quickly the gathering-place and the seats were filled

13
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:05,120
with men assembling; and many marveled at the
sight of Laertes' battle-wise son. For Athena

14
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:10,180
poured a wondrous grace over his head and shoulders
and made him taller to look at, and broader,

15
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:15,840
so that he might be dear to all the Phaeacians,
and dreaded, and revered, and might accomplish the many

16
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:18,940
trials in which the Phaeacians would test Odysseus.

17
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:24,440
And when they were assembled, all in one gathering,
Alcinous addressed them all, and said:

18
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:30,280
"Hear me, leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians,
while I say what the heart in my breast commands.

19
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:34,740
This stranger — I do not know who he is —
has come wandering to my house,

20
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:40,740
whether from the men of the east or of the west;
he presses for a sending, and pleads that it be made sure.

21
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:43,840
Let us then, as always before, speed his sending on.

22
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:49,620
For no man, no — no one who ever comes to my
house waits here long, grieving for his escort.

23
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:53,180
Come: let us haul a black ship
down to the bright salt water,

24
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:55,940
one on her first voyage, and let fifty-two young men

25
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:59,620
be chosen through the town, as many as have proven best.

26
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:04,760
Lash your oars well to the benches, every one,
then come ashore; and afterward come to my house

27
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:08,580
and see to a feast at speed: I will provide well for all.

28
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:14,640
To the young men these are my orders. And you others,
sceptered kings, come to my beautiful house,

29
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:19,840
so we may welcome the stranger in the halls —
let no man refuse. And call the godlike singer,

30
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,580
Demodocus: for to him the god has given song

31
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:26,880
beyond all others, to delight men
however his heart is stirred to sing."

32
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:29,460
So speaking he led the way, and the sceptered kings

33
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,440
followed with him; and a herald
went for the godlike singer.

34
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:37,200
And the fifty-two young men, chosen out, went,
as he commanded, to the beach of the barren sea.

35
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:41,880
And when they had come down to the ship and the sea,
they hauled the black ship down to deep water,

36
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,180
and set the mast and the sails in the black ship,
and fitted the oars into their leather slings,

37
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:49,280
all in due order, and spread the white sails open.

38
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:54,480
They moored her well out in the water, and then
went their way to the great house of wise Alcinous.

39
00:02:54,480 --> 00:03:00,100
And the porticoes and courts and halls were filled
with men assembling: there were many, young and old.

40
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:05,760
For them Alcinous slaughtered twelve sheep,
eight white-tusked hogs, and two shambling oxen;

41
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:09,300
these they flayed and dressed,
and made the lovely feast ready.

42
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,880
And the herald came near, leading the trusted singer,

43
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:17,640
whom the Muse loved beyond other men,
and gave him good and evil both:

44
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:21,220
she took away his eyes, and gave him sweet song.

45
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:27,040
For him Pontonous set a silver-studded chair in the
middle of the feasters, leaning it on a tall pillar,

46
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:31,920
and hung the clear-voiced lyre on its peg just
above his head, and showed him how to reach it

47
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:36,040
with his hands; and set by him
a basket and a beautiful table,

48
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:39,920
and a cup of wine beside,
to drink when his heart should bid him.

49
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:43,140
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

50
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:45,960
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,

51
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:49,420
the Muse stirred the singer to
sing the famous deeds of men,

52
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,180
from that song whose fame had
then already reached wide heaven:

53
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:58,960
the quarrel of Odysseus and Achilles, Peleus' son —
how once they clashed at the gods' rich feast

54
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:01,880
with terrible words, and the lord of men Agamemnon

55
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:05,520
rejoiced in his heart that the best
of the Achaeans were quarreling.

56
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:11,000
For so Phoebus Apollo had told him in prophecy at
holy Pytho, when he crossed the stone threshold

57
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,660
to consult the god: then the
beginning of woe was rolling down

58
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:16,860
on Trojans and Danaans alike.

59
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:22,860
This the famous singer sang. But Odysseus
took his great purple cloak in his heavy hands

60
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:26,800
and drew it down over his head, and hid his handsome face:

61
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:30,960
he was ashamed to let the tears run
under his brows before the Phaeacians.

62
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,040
Whenever the godlike singer paused in his singing,

63
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,820
he would wipe the tears away,
and take the cloak from his head,

64
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:41,080
and lifting the two-handled cup would pour to the gods;

65
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:43,920
but whenever he began again,
and the best of the Phaeacians

66
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:50,660
urged him to sing, because they took joy in the tale,
Odysseus would cover his head again, and mourn.

67
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:56,320
From all the others he hid the tears he shed;
Alcinous alone remarked him and noticed,

68
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,280
sitting beside him, and heard his heavy groaning.

69
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:02,740
At once he spoke among the oar-loving Phaeacians:

70
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:05,820
"Hear me, leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians.

71
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:08,860
By now we have had our hearts' fill of the shared feast,

72
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:12,260
and of the lyre, which is the companion of the rich feast.

73
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:17,700
Now let us go out and try our hands at contests,
all of them — so that the stranger may tell his own people,

74
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:24,860
when he has come home, how far we surpass others at
boxing and wrestling and jumping and speed of foot."

75
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:27,980
So speaking he led the way, and they went with him.

76
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:31,120
And the herald hung the clear-voiced lyre on its peg,

77
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:36,120
took Demodocus by the hand, and led him out of the hall,
guiding him along the same road the others

78
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:40,460
had gone, the best of the Phaeacians,
to wonder at the games.

79
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:44,440
They went their way to the gathering-place,
and a huge crowd followed,

80
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:47,460
thousands; and many fine young men stood up.

81
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:54,400
Up rose Acroneus and Ocyalus and Elatreus,
Nauteus and Prymneus and Anchialus and Eretmeus,

82
00:05:54,400 --> 00:06:01,180
Ponteus and Proreus, Thoon and Anabesineus,
and Amphialus, son of Polyneus, Tecton's son;

83
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:07,400
and up rose Euryalus, the equal of man-destroying Ares,
Naubolus' son, who was best in beauty and body

84
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:10,940
of all the Phaeacians after blameless Laodamas.

85
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:13,880
And up stood the three sons of blameless Alcinous:

86
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:16,940
Laodamas, and Halius, and godlike Clytoneus.

87
00:06:16,940 --> 00:06:20,540
First they made trial of one another in the footrace.

88
00:06:20,540 --> 00:06:23,660
The course was stretched for them from
the turning-post, and all together

89
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:29,940
they flew off quickly, raising the dust of the plain;
and far the best at running was blameless Clytoneus:

90
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:33,460
by as much as the width of a
mule-team's furrow in fallow ground,

91
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:37,740
by that much he outran the rest to
the crowd, and left them behind.

92
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:42,480
Then they made trial of grinding wrestling,
and there Euryalus outdid all the best;

93
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:48,100
at the jump Amphialus was first of them all;
with the discus far the best of all was Elatreus,

94
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:52,000
and at boxing Laodamas, Alcinous' good son.

95
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,200
And when they had all taken their
hearts' pleasure in the contests,

96
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:58,020
Laodamas, Alcinous' son, spoke among them:

97
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:00,660
"Come, friends, let us ask the stranger whether he knows

98
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:05,480
any contest, and has learned it.
In build, at least, he is no mean man —

99
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:10,320
the thighs and calves of him, the two arms above,
the massive neck, the great strength;

100
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:12,780
only he is broken by many evils.

101
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:19,020
For I say there is nothing worse than the
sea to shatter a man, however strong he be."

102
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:22,040
Then Euryalus answered him in turn, and said:

103
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:25,160
"Laodamas, you have spoken this word in due measure.

104
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:28,520
Now go yourself and challenge him, and speak your speech."

105
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:33,880
And when the good son of Alcinous heard it,
he went and stood in the middle, and addressed Odysseus:

106
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:37,480
"Come you too, father stranger:
try your hand at the contests,

107
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:41,200
if you have learned any —
and it is like you to know contests:

108
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:46,520
for there is no greater fame for a man, while he lives,
than what he achieves with his own feet and hands.

109
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:50,000
Come, make a trial; scatter the cares from your heart.

110
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,820
Your road will not be long delayed now: already the
ship is hauled down, and the companions are ready."

111
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:58,340
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

112
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:02,180
"Laodamas, why do you all urge this on me, mocking?

113
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:04,520
Cares are closer to my heart than contests.

114
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:10,560
I have suffered much before now, much have I labored,
and now I sit in your gathering hungry for homecoming,

115
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:12,860
pleading with the king and with all the people."

116
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:16,000
Then Euryalus answered him — and jeered to his face:

117
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,880
"No, stranger: I do not take you for a man of contests,
such as are held in plenty among mankind —

118
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:27,500
rather for one who plies to and fro in a many-benched ship,
a master of sailors who are traders,

119
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:32,860
with a memory for cargo, an eye on his freight
and his grasping profits. You are no athlete."

120
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:38,320
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

121
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:42,640
"Stranger, you have not spoken well.
You are like a man blinded by folly.

122
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:45,760
So true it is that the gods do
not give their graces to all men

123
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:49,540
alike — not build, and brains,
and speaking-skill together.

124
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:54,580
One man is slighter to look at, but the god
crowns his shape with words, and men gaze at him

125
00:08:54,580 --> 00:09:00,740
delighted, while he speaks on without a stumble,
with honeyed modesty, and shines among the gathered people,

126
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:03,980
and when he walks the town they look on him as on a god.

127
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:09,300
Another again is like the immortals in beauty,
but no grace is set like a crown upon his words.

128
00:09:09,300 --> 00:09:15,260
So with you: your looks are striking — not a god
could better them — but your mind is an empty thing.

129
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:20,640
You have stirred the heart in my breast by speaking
out of all measure. I am no novice at contests,

130
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:26,380
whatever you may say. I count myself among the first,
as long as I could trust my youth and these my hands.

131
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:33,260
Now I am held in misery and pain: I have endured much,
cutting through wars of men and the grinding waves.

132
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:37,540
But even so, for all my many evils,
I will try your contests:

133
00:09:37,540 --> 00:09:41,420
your word has bitten to the heart,
and your speech has stung me to it."

134
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:45,220
He spoke, and cloak and all he
sprang up, and seized a discus

135
00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:48,500
bigger than the rest, and thick —
heavier by no small measure

136
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:51,160
than the ones the Phaeacians threw among themselves.

137
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:56,780
He spun and let it fly from his massive hand,
and the stone hummed; and down to the ground they cowered —

138
00:09:56,780 --> 00:10:00,060
the Phaeacians of the long oars, men famed for ships —

139
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:03,400
under the rush of the stone. Past all their marks it flew,

140
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:06,060
running light off his hand. And Athena set the mark,

141
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:09,600
in the likeness of a man, and spoke a word, and named him:

142
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:12,220
"Even a blind man, stranger, could pick out your token,

143
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:14,820
groping — for it is not mixed in with the crowd of them

144
00:10:14,820 --> 00:10:18,300
but far in front. Take heart, at least for this contest:

145
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:21,460
no Phaeacian will reach this, let alone throw beyond it."

146
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:29,100
So he spoke; and much-enduring noble Odysseus was glad,
rejoicing to find one true friend in the field.

147
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:32,560
And then with a lighter heart
he spoke among the Phaeacians:

148
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:39,580
"Now match that, young men — and soon after, I think,
I will send another as far, or farther still.

149
00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:42,660
As for the rest of you —
whoever's heart and spirit bid him,

150
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:45,920
come out and be tried, since you have angered me so:

151
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:52,400
at boxing, wrestling, running — I refuse nothing —
any Phaeacian at all, except Laodamas himself.

152
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:55,840
He is my host: who would fight the man who loves him?

153
00:10:55,840 --> 00:11:00,400
Witless, worth nothing — that is the man who
challenges his own host to a bout of games

154
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:04,380
in a strange country: he cuts himself off at the knees.

155
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:07,720
But of the rest I refuse no one, scorn no one:

156
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:10,980
I want to know you, and be tried face to face.

157
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:14,320
I am not bad at anything — of all the contests men keep.

158
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:17,060
I know well how to handle the polished bow:

159
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:21,980
first of a company I would strike my man with an
arrow in the enemy crowd, though comrades in plenty

160
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:24,800
stood close beside me shooting at the men.

161
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:30,360
Philoctetes alone outdid me with the bow in the
Trojans' country, whenever we Achaeans shot;

162
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:35,900
but of all others I say I am far the best,
all mortals now alive who eat their bread on earth.

163
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:41,280
With the men of the past I will not seek to rival —
not Heracles, not Eurytus of Oechalia,

164
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:43,860
who rivaled the very immortals with the bow.

165
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:49,660
That is why great Eurytus died so soon — old age
never found him in his halls: Apollo in his anger

166
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:52,980
killed him, for daring to challenge him at the bow.

167
00:11:52,980 --> 00:11:56,400
And I hurl a spear as far as another man shoots an arrow.

168
00:11:56,400 --> 00:12:01,700
Only at running I fear one of you might pass me —
one of the Phaeacians: I was broken past all decency

169
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:08,140
among the many waves, with no steady keep aboard ship;
and so the joints of my limbs are loosened."

170
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:11,280
So he spoke, and they were all hushed in silence.

171
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:13,660
Alcinous alone made him this answer:

172
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:17,300
"Stranger, nothing you say among us is said ungraciously:

173
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:19,940
you are willing to show the prowess that attends you,

174
00:12:19,940 --> 00:12:22,980
in anger that this man stood up beside you in the field

175
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:28,640
and taunted you — as no mortal would slight your
prowess who knew in his heart how to speak things fitly.

176
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:33,420
But mark my word now, so that you may tell
it to another hero, when in your own halls

177
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:38,440
you feast beside your wife and your children
and remember our prowess: what works Zeus grants

178
00:12:38,440 --> 00:12:42,120
to us as well, unbroken from our fathers' days.

179
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:44,900
For we are not flawless boxers, nor wrestlers:

180
00:12:44,900 --> 00:12:48,180
but we run swift-footed, and we are best with ships,

181
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:54,540
and always dear to us is the feast, the lyre,
the dances, changes of clothing, hot baths, and beds.

182
00:12:54,540 --> 00:13:00,140
Come then, dancers of the Phaeacians, all your best,
and play — so the stranger may tell his own people,

183
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:05,660
when he has come home, how far we surpass others
in seamanship and running and dance and song.

184
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:11,500
And let someone go quickly and fetch Demodocus the
clear-voiced lyre, which lies somewhere in our halls."

185
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:18,280
So spoke godlike Alcinous; and a herald rose
to bring the hollow lyre from the king's house.

186
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:24,500
And chosen stewards stood up, nine in all, public men,
who at the gatherings set each thing in order:

187
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:28,120
they smoothed a dancing-floor and widened a fine ring.

188
00:13:28,120 --> 00:13:31,100
And the herald came near, bearing the clear-voiced lyre

189
00:13:31,100 --> 00:13:35,040
for Demodocus; and he stepped to
the middle, and around him stood

190
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:38,140
boys in their first youth, skilled in the dance,

191
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:41,440
and they beat the shining floor
with their feet. And Odysseus

192
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:44,920
gazed at the flickerings of their feet,
and marveled in his heart.

193
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:48,560
And the singer, striking the lyre,
struck up a beautiful song

194
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:52,460
of the love of Ares and Aphrodite of the lovely crown:

195
00:13:52,460 --> 00:13:55,400
how first they lay together in the house of Hephaestus,

196
00:13:55,400 --> 00:14:00,020
in secret; and Ares gave her many gifts,
and shamed the marriage-bed

197
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:04,000
of the lord Hephaestus. But to him came a messenger —

198
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,980
Helios, the Sun, who had seen
the two of them lying in love.

199
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:10,720
And when Hephaestus heard the heart-stinging tale,

200
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:14,860
he went his way to his forge,
brooding evils deep in his heart,

201
00:14:14,860 --> 00:14:18,180
and set the great anvil on the
block, and hammered out bonds

202
00:14:18,180 --> 00:14:22,140
that could not break or slip,
to hold them fast where they stood.

203
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:25,460
And when he had made his snare, in his fury against Ares,

204
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:29,040
he went his way to the chamber where his own bed stood,

205
00:14:29,040 --> 00:14:32,880
and around the bedposts he poured
the bonds in a circle everywhere,

206
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:39,520
and many hung down from above, from the roof-beam,
fine as spider-webs, which no one could so much see —

207
00:14:39,520 --> 00:14:43,680
not even the blessed gods:
for they were made past all cunning.

208
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:46,560
And when he had poured his whole snare around the bed,

209
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:52,680
he made as if to go to Lemnos, that strong-founded city,
which is dearest to him by far of all lands.

210
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:56,280
And Ares of the golden reins kept no blind watch:

211
00:14:56,280 --> 00:15:02,420
he saw Hephaestus, famed for craft, going off,
and went his way to the house of famous Hephaestus,

212
00:15:02,420 --> 00:15:05,900
hungry for the love of Cytherea of the lovely crown.

213
00:15:05,900 --> 00:15:09,140
She was newly come from her father,
the mighty son of Cronos,

214
00:15:09,140 --> 00:15:11,820
and had just sat down; and he came inside the house

215
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:15,320
and clamped a hand on hers,
and spoke a word, and named her:

216
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:19,860
"Come, love — to bed, and take
our joy together lying close:

217
00:15:19,860 --> 00:15:25,920
Hephaestus is no longer at home; by now, I think,
he is off to Lemnos, among the wild-tongued Sintians."

218
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:29,480
So he spoke; and welcome it seemed to her to lie with him.

219
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:32,560
So the two went to the bed and
lay down; and around them poured

220
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:35,640
the artful bonds of Hephaestus of the many designs,

221
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:38,840
and they could not move a limb,
could not lift themselves —

222
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:41,680
and then they knew it: there was no escaping now.

223
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,320
And near them came the famous god of the two strong arms,

224
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:47,860
turned back on his road before ever reaching Lemnos:

225
00:15:47,860 --> 00:15:51,360
Helios had kept the watch for him, and told him the tale.

226
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:54,660
He went his way to his house, his heart heavy within him,

227
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:57,660
and stood in the gateway, and savage anger gripped him,

228
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:01,040
and he cried out terribly, and called to all the gods:

229
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:03,820
"Father Zeus, and you other blessed gods who are forever:

230
00:16:03,820 --> 00:16:10,000
come here — see something for laughter, deeds past
bearing — how Aphrodite, Zeus' daughter, forever

231
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:13,820
dishonors me for being lame, and loves that destroyer Ares

232
00:16:13,820 --> 00:16:19,360
because he is handsome and straight of limb, while I
was born crippled. And whose fault is that? No one's

233
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:22,880
but my two parents' — I wish they had never bred me!

234
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:29,040
Look, see where the two of them sleep in love,
climbed into my own bed. And I watch, and it eats my heart.

235
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:32,180
Yet I do not think they will
want to lie so one minute longer,

236
00:16:32,180 --> 00:16:35,460
however much in love:
soon enough the two of them will lose

237
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:39,260
their taste for sleeping. But the
snare and the bond will hold them

238
00:16:39,260 --> 00:16:45,160
until her father pays me back the bride-gifts,
every one, all I handed over for his bitch-eyed girl —

239
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:48,680
because his daughter is beautiful,
and cannot hold her heart."

240
00:16:48,680 --> 00:16:52,640
So he spoke, and the gods gathered
to the bronze-floored house:

241
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:58,660
Poseidon came, who holds the earth; and the runner
Hermes came; and the lord who works from far, Apollo.

242
00:16:58,660 --> 00:17:02,360
But the goddesses stayed at home,
for shame, every one of them.

243
00:17:02,360 --> 00:17:06,080
So they stood in the gateway,
the gods, the givers of good things,

244
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:09,200
and unquenchable laughter rose among the blessed gods

245
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:13,440
as they gazed on the craft of
Hephaestus of the many designs.

246
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:16,140
And thus one would say, with a glance at his neighbor:

247
00:17:16,140 --> 00:17:20,000
"Bad deeds do not prosper.
The slow one catches the swift —

248
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:27,460
look, Hephaestus now, slow as he is, has taken Ares,
the swiftest of the gods who hold Olympus:

249
00:17:27,460 --> 00:17:31,820
lame, and by craft. Now the adulterer's fine is owing."

250
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:33,760
So they spoke such things to one another.

251
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:38,380
But the lord Apollo, son of Zeus, said to Hermes:

252
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:41,340
"Hermes, son of Zeus, guide, giver of good things:

253
00:17:41,340 --> 00:17:47,860
would you be willing, pressed hard in strong bonds,
to sleep in that bed beside golden Aphrodite?"

254
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:50,740
Then the guide, the slayer of Argus, answered him:

255
00:17:50,740 --> 00:17:54,380
"If only that could be, lord Apollo of the far arrows!

256
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:57,740
Let three times as many bonds hold me, past all number,

257
00:17:57,740 --> 00:18:00,840
and you gods look on — yes, and all the goddesses too:

258
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:03,820
I would still sleep beside golden Aphrodite."

259
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:07,420
So he spoke, and laughter rose among the deathless gods.

260
00:18:07,420 --> 00:18:13,780
But Poseidon did not laugh; he kept pleading with
Hephaestus, the famous worker, to set Ares free,

261
00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:17,000
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:

262
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:23,240
"Release him. I promise you, before the deathless gods,
he shall pay all that is fair, as you demand."

263
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:26,200
Then the famous god of the two strong arms answered him:

264
00:18:26,200 --> 00:18:29,620
"Do not ask this of me, Poseidon who hold the earth.

265
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:32,660
Worthless the pledges you take for worthless men.

266
00:18:32,660 --> 00:18:39,200
How could I hold you bound before the deathless gods
if Ares slips both debt and bond and goes his way?"

267
00:18:39,200 --> 00:18:41,660
Then Poseidon the earth-shaker answered him:

268
00:18:41,660 --> 00:18:47,140
"Hephaestus, if Ares does slip his debt and
vanishes, I will pay you the fine myself."

269
00:18:47,140 --> 00:18:50,080
Then the famous god of the two strong arms replied:

270
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:54,360
"It cannot be, and it would not be
right, that I refuse your word."

271
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:57,720
So speaking, the strength of Hephaestus undid the bond.

272
00:18:57,720 --> 00:19:04,280
And the two of them, loosed from the bond,
strong as it was, sprang up at once: he was gone to Thrace,

273
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:07,360
and she came to Cyprus, laughter-loving Aphrodite —

274
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:11,820
to Paphos, where her precinct is,
and her altar fragrant with smoke.

275
00:19:11,820 --> 00:19:14,880
There the Graces bathed her and rubbed her with oil,

276
00:19:14,880 --> 00:19:18,140
the immortal oil that blooms on the gods who are forever,

277
00:19:18,140 --> 00:19:21,540
and dressed her in lovely clothing, a wonder to see.

278
00:19:21,540 --> 00:19:27,540
This the famous singer sang; and Odysseus took delight
in his heart as he listened, and so did the others,

279
00:19:27,540 --> 00:19:31,340
the Phaeacians of the long oars, men famed for ships.

280
00:19:31,340 --> 00:19:37,740
Then Alcinous told Halius and Laodamas to
dance alone, since no one could rival them.

281
00:19:37,740 --> 00:19:43,960
And when they had taken in their hands the beautiful ball,
purple, which wise Polybus had made them,

282
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:47,860
one would bend far back and hurl
it up toward the shadowy clouds,

283
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:51,280
and the other, leaping high off the ground, would catch it

284
00:19:51,280 --> 00:19:54,640
lightly, with ease, before his feet came back to earth.

285
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:58,200
And when they had tried their skill
at the ball thrown straight up,

286
00:19:58,200 --> 00:20:01,180
then the two of them danced on the nourishing earth,

287
00:20:01,180 --> 00:20:04,220
passing it swiftly back and forth; and the other young men

288
00:20:04,220 --> 00:20:08,320
beat time, standing around the ring,
and a great din went up.

289
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:11,240
And then noble Odysseus addressed Alcinous:

290
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:16,400
"Lord Alcinous, most renowned among all peoples,
you promised your dancers were the best,

291
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:21,220
and here it stands accomplished.
Awe takes me, looking at it."

292
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:25,060
So he spoke; and the sacred strength of Alcinous was glad,

293
00:20:25,060 --> 00:20:28,180
and at once he spoke among the oar-loving Phaeacians:

294
00:20:28,180 --> 00:20:30,980
"Hear me, leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians.

295
00:20:30,980 --> 00:20:34,200
This stranger seems to me a man of clear-headed sense.

296
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:37,440
Come, let us give him a guest-gift, as is fitting.

297
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:43,040
Twelve glorious kings hold sway through this
land as leaders, and I myself am the thirteenth:

298
00:20:43,040 --> 00:20:47,900
let each of you bring a fresh-washed mantle
and a tunic and a talent of precious gold;

299
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:50,900
and let us bring it all together
at once, so that the stranger

300
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:53,980
may take it in his hands and go to supper glad at heart.

301
00:20:53,980 --> 00:20:56,940
And let Euryalus make it up to him, man to man, with words

302
00:20:56,940 --> 00:21:00,640
and with a gift: for his word
was not spoken in due measure."

303
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:06,540
So he spoke, and they all approved it and urged it on,
and each sent off a herald to bring his gifts.

304
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:09,180
And Euryalus answered him in turn, and said:

305
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:15,140
"Lord Alcinous, most renowned among all peoples,
I will make it up to the stranger, as you command.

306
00:21:15,140 --> 00:21:21,000
I will give him this sword, all bronze, with a hilt
of silver on it, and a scabbard of fresh-sawn ivory

307
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:24,200
turned round it: it will be worth much to him."

308
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:30,780
So saying he set the silver-studded sword in his hands,
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:

309
00:21:30,780 --> 00:21:33,660
"Hail, father stranger. If some word was blurted out

310
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:36,900
too harsh, may the storm-winds
snatch it up and carry it away.

311
00:21:36,900 --> 00:21:40,280
And may the gods grant you to see your wife, and to come

312
00:21:40,280 --> 00:21:45,240
to your fatherland: for long, far from
your own people, you have suffered pains."

313
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:47,860
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

314
00:21:47,860 --> 00:21:51,840
"And to you, friend: hail, and warmly.
May the gods give you fortune,

315
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:54,620
and may you never feel the lack, hereafter, of this sword

316
00:21:54,620 --> 00:21:57,940
you have given me — you who made it up to me with words."

317
00:21:57,940 --> 00:22:01,840
He spoke, and slung the silver-studded
sword about his shoulders.

318
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:05,780
And the sun went down, and the
famous gifts lay there before him.

319
00:22:05,780 --> 00:22:11,560
The lordly heralds carried them to the house of Alcinous,
and the sons of blameless Alcinous received them

320
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:13,920
and set the beautiful gifts before their honored mother.

321
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:20,060
And the sacred strength of Alcinous led the way,
and they came and sat down on the high seats.

322
00:22:20,060 --> 00:22:23,080
And then the strength of Alcinous said to Arete:

323
00:22:23,080 --> 00:22:26,940
"Bring here a glorious chest, wife — the best one —

324
00:22:26,940 --> 00:22:30,480
and set in it yourself a fresh-washed mantle and a tunic.

325
00:22:30,480 --> 00:22:34,000
And heat the bronze cauldron
over the fire, and warm water,

326
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:37,220
so that once he has bathed,
and seen well laid together all

327
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:40,500
the gifts the blameless Phaeacians have brought him here,

328
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:44,400
he may take joy in the feast,
and in hearing the singer's strain.

329
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:49,580
And I will give him this beautiful cup of mine,
golden, so that remembering me all his days

330
00:22:49,580 --> 00:22:52,880
he may pour in his hall to Zeus and the other gods."

331
00:22:52,880 --> 00:22:59,600
So he spoke, and Arete told her slave-women to set a
great three-legged cauldron on the fire with all speed.

332
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:05,760
They set the bath-cauldron on the blazing fire and
poured water in, and fed the wood beneath till it flamed:

333
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:09,740
the fire wrapped round the belly of
the cauldron, and the water warmed.

334
00:23:09,740 --> 00:23:12,680
Meanwhile Arete brought out from
her chamber a beautiful chest

335
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:18,480
for the stranger, and laid the fine gifts in it,
the clothing and the gold the Phaeacians had given,

336
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,880
and in with them she laid a mantle and a beautiful tunic,

337
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:25,140
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to him:

338
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:28,840
"See to the lid yourself now,
and tie the knot on it quickly,

339
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:31,640
for fear someone rob you on the voyage, when later on

340
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:34,780
you sleep your sweet sleep as the black ship carries you."

341
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:40,220
And when much-enduring noble Odysseus heard it,
at once he fitted the lid, and quickly tied upon it

342
00:23:40,220 --> 00:23:43,380
the intricate knot that the lady Circe once taught him.

343
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:46,480
And straightway the housekeeper called him to his bath,

344
00:23:46,480 --> 00:23:52,100
to step in the tub; and his heart was glad at the sight
of the hot water: for he had known no such tending

345
00:23:52,100 --> 00:23:54,940
since he left the house of fair-haired Calypso —

346
00:23:54,940 --> 00:23:58,400
though while he was there,
he was tended like a god without fail.

347
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:01,700
And when the slave-women had
bathed him and rubbed him with oil,

348
00:24:01,700 --> 00:24:04,380
and thrown a beautiful cloak around him, and a tunic,

349
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:08,180
he stepped from the bath and went
to join the men at their wine.

350
00:24:08,180 --> 00:24:13,260
And Nausicaa, with her beauty from the gods,
stood by the pillar of the close-built roof,

351
00:24:13,260 --> 00:24:19,360
and marveled at Odysseus as her eyes took him in,
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to him:

352
00:24:19,360 --> 00:24:22,580
"Farewell, stranger. And sometime, in your own fatherland,

353
00:24:22,580 --> 00:24:26,420
remember me — for to me first
you owe the price of your life."

354
00:24:26,420 --> 00:24:29,400
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

355
00:24:29,400 --> 00:24:32,240
"Nausicaa, daughter of great-hearted Alcinous:

356
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:36,160
only may Zeus grant it so —
the loud-thundering lord of Hera —

357
00:24:36,160 --> 00:24:39,220
that I go home and look upon my day of homecoming:

358
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:45,340
then even there I would pray to you as to a god,
always, all my days. For you saved my life, girl."

359
00:24:45,340 --> 00:24:49,100
He spoke, and went to his seat beside King Alcinous.

360
00:24:49,100 --> 00:24:52,180
And already they were serving
portions and mixing the wine,

361
00:24:52,180 --> 00:24:57,720
and the herald came near, leading the trusted singer,
Demodocus, honored among the people, and seated him

362
00:24:57,720 --> 00:25:01,400
in the middle of the feasters,
leaning him on a tall pillar.

363
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:04,520
And then Odysseus of the many designs called the herald —

364
00:25:04,520 --> 00:25:07,720
cutting off a slice of the chine, with more left over,

365
00:25:07,720 --> 00:25:12,140
of the white-tusked hog, rich fat along it either side:

366
00:25:12,140 --> 00:25:13,840
"Herald — here, take this cut of meat to Demodocus,

367
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:19,120
so he may eat; and I will pay my
respects to him, for all my grief.

368
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:23,960
For among all men upon the earth, singers have
their share of honor and reverence, because the Muse

369
00:25:23,960 --> 00:25:27,580
has taught them her paths,
and loves the tribe of singers."

370
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:30,860
So he spoke, and the herald
carried it and set it in the hands

371
00:25:30,860 --> 00:25:35,440
of the hero Demodocus; and he received it,
and his heart was glad.

372
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:38,720
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

373
00:25:38,720 --> 00:25:45,540
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
then Odysseus of the many designs said to Demodocus:

374
00:25:45,540 --> 00:25:48,840
"Demodocus, I praise you beyond all mortal men.

375
00:25:48,840 --> 00:25:52,000
Either the Muse taught you, Zeus' daughter, or Apollo:

376
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:55,720
for all in due order you sing the fate of the Achaeans —

377
00:25:55,720 --> 00:25:58,940
all they did and suffered, all the Achaeans' labors —

378
00:25:58,940 --> 00:26:02,720
as if you had been there yourself,
or heard it from one who was.

379
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:05,880
But come now, change your path:
sing the making of the horse

380
00:26:05,880 --> 00:26:09,340
of wood, which Epeius built, and Athena with him —

381
00:26:09,340 --> 00:26:15,040
the snare that noble Odysseus brought up into the
citadel, filled with the men who sacked Ilium.

382
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:19,980
If you tell me this tale too in its due measure,
then I will proclaim at once to all mankind

383
00:26:19,980 --> 00:26:23,460
how the god of his grace has given you divine song."

384
00:26:23,460 --> 00:26:28,800
So he spoke; and the singer, stirred by
the god, began, and made the song appear,

385
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:31,740
taking it up where the Argives had
boarded their well-benched ships

386
00:26:31,740 --> 00:26:38,560
and were sailing away, after casting fire on their camp,
while those others, around famous Odysseus, already

387
00:26:38,560 --> 00:26:42,260
sat in the Trojans' gathering-place,
hidden inside the horse:

388
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:45,540
for the Trojans themselves had
dragged it up into the citadel.

389
00:26:45,540 --> 00:26:48,960
So it stood there, and they talked on and on, undecided,

390
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:52,220
sitting around it; and three
ways the counsel pleased them:

391
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:57,720
to split the hollow timber with the pitiless bronze,
or drag it to the height and hurl it down the rocks,

392
00:26:57,720 --> 00:27:03,440
or leave it standing, a great offering to appease the
gods — and that was the way it was to end at last:

393
00:27:03,440 --> 00:27:06,460
for it was their portion to perish
when the city took within it

394
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:12,000
the great horse of wood, where all the best of the
Argives sat carrying death and doom to the Trojans.

395
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:15,040
And he sang how the sons of the Achaeans sacked the city,

396
00:27:15,040 --> 00:27:18,140
pouring from the horse, leaving their hollow ambush;

397
00:27:18,140 --> 00:27:21,900
he sang how they ravaged the
steep city, each man his own way,

398
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:28,520
and how Odysseus went, like Ares himself,
to the house of Deiphobus, with godlike Menelaus beside him;

399
00:27:28,520 --> 00:27:31,500
and there, he sang, he dared the grimmest fight of all —

400
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:34,220
and conquered, in the end, through great-hearted Athena.

401
00:27:34,220 --> 00:27:42,660
This the famous singer sang. And Odysseus melted:
the tears from under his eyelids wet his cheeks.

402
00:27:42,660 --> 00:27:49,040
As a woman weeps, fallen on the body of her dear husband
who has gone down before his city and his people,

403
00:27:49,040 --> 00:27:51,960
warding the pitiless day from his town and his children:

404
00:27:51,960 --> 00:27:59,240
she sees him dying, gasping his last, and pours
herself around him with a high thin cry; and behind her

405
00:27:59,240 --> 00:28:02,960
the spearmen, beating her back
and shoulders with their spears,

406
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:09,980
lead her away into slavery, to labor and misery,
and her cheeks are wasted with a grief most pitiful:

407
00:28:09,980 --> 00:28:14,240
so pitiful were the tears Odysseus
let fall from under his brows.

408
00:28:14,240 --> 00:28:19,120
From all the others he hid the tears he shed;
Alcinous alone remarked him and noticed,

409
00:28:19,120 --> 00:28:22,080
sitting beside him, and heard his heavy groaning.

410
00:28:22,080 --> 00:28:25,400
At once he spoke among the oar-loving Phaeacians:

411
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:31,620
"Hear me, leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians —
and let Demodocus still his clear-voiced lyre now:

412
00:28:31,620 --> 00:28:34,680
for what he sings does not give joy to all alike.

413
00:28:34,680 --> 00:28:39,760
Since the hour we supped, and the godlike singer rose,
since then the stranger has never ceased

414
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:44,520
his grieving lament. Great sorrow,
surely, has fenced his heart around.

415
00:28:44,520 --> 00:28:51,520
Come, let the singer hold, so that we may all take
pleasure alike, hosts and guest together: far better so.

416
00:28:51,520 --> 00:28:54,080
For all this has been made ready for our honored guest —

417
00:28:54,080 --> 00:28:57,460
his sending, and the friendly
gifts we give him in our love.

418
00:28:57,460 --> 00:29:02,980
As dear as a brother is the stranger and the suppliant
to any man whose wits have even a little reach.

419
00:29:02,980 --> 00:29:09,560
So do not hide now, behind shrewd calculations,
whatever I ask you. To speak out is the fairer way.

420
00:29:09,560 --> 00:29:12,540
Tell the name they call you there by,
your mother and your father

421
00:29:12,540 --> 00:29:15,900
and the others, in the town and dwelling round about.

422
00:29:15,900 --> 00:29:21,860
For no one among mankind is altogether nameless,
base man or noble, once he has come into the world:

423
00:29:21,860 --> 00:29:25,000
parents lay a name on every child they bear.

424
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,820
And tell me your land, your people, and your city,

425
00:29:27,820 --> 00:29:31,700
so that our ships may carry you there,
plotting the course by thought:

426
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:37,180
for the Phaeacians have no steersmen,
no steering-oars at all, such as other ships carry:

427
00:29:37,180 --> 00:29:41,780
the ships themselves know the thoughts and minds of men,
and know the cities and rich fields of every

428
00:29:41,780 --> 00:29:46,100
people, and swiftest of all things
cross the gulf of the salt sea

429
00:29:46,100 --> 00:29:51,020
wrapped in mist and cloud; and never do
they fear harm or wreck on any crossing.

430
00:29:51,020 --> 00:29:56,180
Yet this I once heard my father Nausithous say:
he would tell us that Poseidon looked on us with anger

431
00:29:56,180 --> 00:29:59,420
because we are safe escorts of
all men without distinction;

432
00:29:59,420 --> 00:30:02,840
and he said that one day,
as a well-made ship of the Phaeacian men

433
00:30:02,840 --> 00:30:06,100
came home over the misted sea from such a sending,

434
00:30:06,100 --> 00:30:10,280
the god would smash it, and pile
a great mountain round our city.

435
00:30:10,280 --> 00:30:13,620
So the old man said. And these
things the god may accomplish,

436
00:30:13,620 --> 00:30:17,280
or may leave unaccomplished, as pleases his own heart.

437
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:20,760
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

438
00:30:20,760 --> 00:30:26,260
where have you wandered? To what countries of men have
you come — the peoples, the cities well-lived-in —

439
00:30:26,260 --> 00:30:33,480
both the harsh among them, the savage and unjust,
and the kind to strangers, whose hearts fear the gods?

440
00:30:33,480 --> 00:30:36,220
And tell why you weep, and mourn within your heart,

441
00:30:36,220 --> 00:30:39,240
when you hear the fate of the Argive Danaans and of Ilium.

442
00:30:39,240 --> 00:30:42,840
That fate the gods made;
they spun that destruction for men,

443
00:30:42,840 --> 00:30:46,100
so that there might be a song for those still to come.

444
00:30:46,100 --> 00:30:48,520
Did some kinsman of yours die before Ilium —

445
00:30:48,520 --> 00:30:55,280
a good man, a wife's father, a daughter's husband? —
those who stand closest after our own blood and stock;

446
00:30:55,280 --> 00:31:00,620
or perhaps a companion, a man dear to your heart,
a good man? For no less than a brother

447
00:31:00,620 --> 00:31:03,122
is a companion who is clear-headed and true."
